{"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-30","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"30","title":"Providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"Phasedown of Hydrofluorocarbons: Management of Certain Hydrofluorocarbons and Substitutes Under the American Innovation and Manufacturing Act of 2020\".","introducedDate":"2025-01-24","latestActionDate":"2025-01-24","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Energy and Commerce.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"D000628","cosponsorBioguideIds":["O000175","R000619","M001233","W000821"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution nullifies the Environmental Protection Agency rule titled Phasedown of Hydrofluorocarbons: Management of Certain Hydrofluorocarbons and Substitutes Under the American Innovation and Manufacturing Act of 2020 and published on October 11, 2024. The rule establishes an emission reduction and reclamation program for the management of hydrofluorocarbons, which are greenhouse gases. The rule also establishes alternative Resource Conservation and Recovery Act standards for certain ignitable spent refrigerants being recycled for reuse.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/30","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hjres/BILLSTATUS-119hjres30.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:18.785Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hjres-30","plainSummary":"This joint resolution would nullify an Environmental Protection Agency rule published in October 2024 that manages hydrofluorocarbons, which are greenhouse gases used in refrigeration and other applications. The EPA rule created a program to reduce emissions and reclaim these substances, and set recycling standards for certain spent refrigerants. If this resolution passes, that rule would be canceled and would have no legal effect.","industries":[{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Manufacturers of refrigeration equipment, air conditioning systems, and products using hydrofluorocarbons face direct regulatory obligations under the nullified rule."},{"industry":"waste-water","confidence":0.8,"rationale":"The rule establishes recycling and reclamation standards for spent refrigerants, directly affecting waste management and recycling operations."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Electric utilities using refrigeration and cooling systems in power generation may be subject to the hydrofluorocarbon management requirements."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Building contractors and HVAC installers work with refrigeration systems and would be affected by standards governing hydrofluorocarbon handling and disposal."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"cd3a7bea658928cc3821a06b","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:55:40.032Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would cancel the EPA rule titled Phasedown of Hydrofluorocarbons: Management of Certain Hydrofluorocarbons and Substitutes Under the American Innovation and Manufacturing Act of 2020 and published on October 11, 2024.","whoItTouches":"People living near whatever is being regulated.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in the air, water and land where you live. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-hjres-30","bioguideId":"W000821","cycle":2026,"score":0.021805821091521793,"matches":[{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":140000,"donorShare":0.07108425669751804,"billConfidence":0.6,"contributi